which we'll get into.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.240
But wait, if I'm a CMS actuary

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:05.760
looking at this spreadsheet,

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.880
why isn't someone just hit a manual override?

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:09.120
Like in statistical modeling,

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.680
you can normalize for anomalies.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:11.680
You'd think so, yeah.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.720
Right, so why couldn't they just adjust the math

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:16.040
instead of waiting for a massive political coalition

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:17.120
to force their hand?

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.280
Well, because CMS actuaries are bound by section 1853

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:22.680
of the Social Security Act.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:23.680
The statute.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.680
Right, they can't just invent a new payment tier

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:27.880
because they feel like it

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.520
or because the data looks weird.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.960
They have to follow the statutory formula

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:34.600
and that formula ties MA rates

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:38.440
directly to fee-for-service or FFS per capita costs

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:40.520
in that specific geographic area.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:42.800
Meaning they're legally forced

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:44.960
to look at the traditional Medicare baseline.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:45.800
Exactly.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:48.880
But here is where the math completely detaches from reality.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.760
The dossier calls this the success paradox.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:52.880
The success paradox.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:56.120
Yeah, so Puerto Rico's MA market is incredibly dense.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:59.680
Over 670,000 seniors are in MA.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.120
Which is like what, 96% of the Medicare

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:03.640
eligible population on the island?

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:05.560
Yes, roughly 96%.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:08.000
Leaving a residual fee-for-service population

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.120
of only about 29,000 people.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:11.480
So just 3%.

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.000
Just 3% of beneficiaries.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:14.840
Okay, so let me see if I have this right.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:18.440
Imagine trying to figure out the average grocery budget

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.360
for a town of 100,000 people.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:22.680
And 96% of the town shops

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:26.560
at this hyper-efficient bulk discount Costco.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:28.920
But your federal formula mandates

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:32.480
that you can only calculate the town's entire grocery budget

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.480
by analyzing the spending habits

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.280
of the 3% of people who exclusively buy their food

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:38.880
at a gas station at 2 in the morning.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.840
That is actually a brilliant way to look at it.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.320
It's an inherently corrupted data pool.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.280
You are projecting the complex care costs

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.960
of over 670,000 seniors

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:52.000
based on a highly irregular 29,000-person sample size.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.400
And that sample size doesn't reflect the norm at all.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:55.240
Not at all.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:57.680
The composition of that 29,000-person sample

00:06:57.680 --> 00:06:59.280
is highly atypical.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.640
It disproportionately includes individuals

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:05.160
with alternative coverage like veterans using the VA system

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:08.200
or dual-eligible with highly-fractured claims data.

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:09.280
So they aren't even using

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:11.280
the fee-for-service system in a standard way.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:12.320
Exactly.

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:15.320
Their FFS claims profile doesn't remotely resemble

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:17.920
the actual morbidity or utilization rates

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:19.800
of the broader senior population.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.920
So because the statutory formula relies

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:25.040
on this corrupted low-utilization baseline,

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:28.280
the FFS cost estimates systemically understate

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:30.320
the true cost of delivering care.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.560
Which is why the MA benchmarks are 41%

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:34.840
below the national average

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.880
and frankly 20% below even the U.S. Virgin Islands.

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:38.720
Wow.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:40.760
So they are getting punished literally

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:43.080
because they were too successful

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:45.120
at enrolling seniors into managed care.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:46.120
That's the paradox.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:50.040
The better MA performs, the more the FFS pool shrinks.

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:51.800
And the more distorted the data gets.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:52.640
Right.

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:53.880
And then the lower the benchmark drops.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:56.680
It is basically an actuarial death spiral.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:58.680
And this is why the dossier frames this

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:01.000
as Puerto Rico as Medicare's future.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:01.840
Yes.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:03.800
What's fascinating here is that the mainland

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:05.800
